+## Unsigned Linux local package
+
+On Linux x64, build a local `.deb` together with an AppImage after `pnpm install`:
+
+```sh
+pnpm run package:desktop:linux:x64:unsigned
+```
+
+No `.env` file or signing configuration is required; the command uses the same local defaults as unsigned macOS builds, including the `com.deepseek.harness` app ID. Signed Linux builds, release records, and update feeds do not exist for this target, so the packaging entry rejects it without `--unsigned`. The artifacts land in `apps/desktop/.desktop-build/targets/linux-x64/unsigned-artifacts/` as `deepseek-harness-<version>-linux-amd64-unsigned.deb` and `deepseek-harness-<version>-linux-x86_64-unsigned.AppImage`, using the distribution architecture names; a successful run prints `DEB:` with the absolute path. Install the package with `sudo apt install <path-to-deb>`, or inspect its control fields and file list with `dpkg-deb -I` and `dpkg-deb -c`. Packaging needs access to npm, GitHub release assets, and nodejs.org to fetch Electron and the primary runtime; preparation and the assembled application still run runtime integrity and smoke checks. The Linux application runs the bundled dsh tree under the standalone Node from `resources/runtime/primary-runtime` instead of Electron's Node mode, because the Electron executable loads GTK-linked libraries that crash glib-linked native modules such as sharp ([electron#46323](https://github.com/electron/electron/issues/46323)); the installed Electron binary still owns the desktop shell itself. Closing the window quits the Linux application — there is no tray or Dock icon to reach a hidden instance — and a running-task confirmation cancels back to the visible window.
